<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) || (\old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i) == null));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))) || (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null && this.get(i) == null));</code>

However, there seems to be a slight misunderstanding in the interpretation of the comment. The comment suggests that each element from the specified index to the old size of the list should shift right by one position, meaning the element at position `i` should be equal to the element at position `i+1` in the old list. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (i == \old(this.size())-1 ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))));</code>

This ensures that each element from the specified index to the second last element in the old list shifts right by one position, and the last element becomes null.
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))) || (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null && this.get(i) == null));</code>

This assert statement captures the condition that if the index is within the valid range (0 to the initial size of the list inclusive), then for every valid index from the specified index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null if the old element at this index was null, or equal to the old element at this index.
